    "id": "evt_7b3c582d18934a2cae07a231382925c4",
    "kind": "plan",
    "source": "agent",
    "timestamp": "2026-06-11T03:33:57.952373Z",
    "schema_version": 1,
    "seq": 8,
    "meta": {},
    "summary": "Build a sensor dashboard: Python API (port 3000) serving CSV as JSON, and a Vite+React frontend (port 8000) with a data table and summary card.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "title": "Create Python backend API on port 3000",
        "detail": "Write a single-file Flask app (server.py) that reads uploads/sensor_readings.csv, parses it with the csv module, and serves it as JSON at GET /api/readings. Copy the CSV into the project root so it's accessible."
      },
      {
        "title": "Scaffold Vite + React frontend",
        "detail": "Use `npm create vite@latest frontend -- --template react` to scaffold, then install dependencies. Configure Vite proxy so /api requests forward to port 3000."
      },
      {
        "title": "Build React dashboard components",
        "detail": "Create App.jsx that fetches /api/readings on mount, renders a SummaryCard (count, min temp, max temp) and a DataTable showing all readings. Add basic CSS for clean layout."
      },
      {
        "title": "Start both servers and verify in browser",
        "detail": "Start the Flask backend on port 3000, then start the Vite dev server on port 8000. Verify the page loads, the table populates, and the summary card shows correct count/min/max values."
      }
    ],
    "revision": 1,
    "context": "## What I found\n\n- **CSV structure**: 3 columns (`timestamp`, `sensor`, `temperature_c`), ~200 rows of hardware sensor readings (NVMe, AMD GPU, CPU temps).\n- **Workspace**: Empty except for `uploads/sensor_readings.csv`.\n\n## Approach\n\n- **Backend**: Flask (lightweight, no extra deps beyond `flask` itself). Single `server.py` file that parses CSV on each request and returns JSON. Runs on port 3000.\n- **Frontend**: Vite + React (as requested). Uses Vite's built-in proxy config to forward `/api/*` to `http://localhost:3000`, so the dev server on port 8000 can fetch `/api/readings` without CORS issues.\n- **Summary card**: Computes count of readings, minimum temperature, and maximum temperature from the fetched data.\n- **Table**: Renders all rows with timestamp, sensor name, and temperature.\n\n## Trade-offs\n\n- Flask over Node/Express: Python is already available and the CSV parsing is trivial with the stdlib `csv` module. Either would work fine.\n- No pagination: ~200 rows is small enough to render all at once.\n- CSV re-read on each request: Simple and correct for this scale; no caching needed."
  },
